{
  "schema_version": 1,
  "name": "rainy_delivery",
  "title": "Rainy weekday, delivery-dominant: walk-in demand falls, rider orders take over",
  "provenance": {
    "source": "Synthetic, anchored on the Course 5006 parameter set and on teaching-case ranges for rainy-day channel shift",
    "method": "Flat-ish baseline demand with a mild lunch bump; half of all arrivals are delivery orders (no balking, higher icy/mixed mix, top queue priority), the walk-in half has low patience; two ice slots because cold drinks dominate on a wet day",
    "uncertainty": "high",
    "notes": "Not field-measured. Rain is modelled only as a channel mix shift, not as a change in total demand. The delivery class carries a heavier mixed/iced order mix, which raises make work per customer relative to a dry walk-in day. Delivery orders never balk and never renege by construction of the engine."
  },
